      mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
      inLanguage: "en",
      datePublished: guide.published,
      dateModified: guide.updated ?? guide.published,
      author: { "@id": `${site}/#person` },
      publisher: { "@id": `${site}/#person` },
      image: `${site}/images/case-studies/lofoten-coast-og.jpg`,
      audience: { "@type": "BusinessAudience", audienceType: guide.audience },
      proficiencyLevel: "Beginner",
      about: [
        { "@type": "Product", name: "GCash", category: "Mobile wallet" },
        { "@type": "Country", name: "Philippines" },
        { "@type": "Thing", name: "Payment failure diagnosis" },
      ],
      keywords: guide.tags.join(", "),
      citation: sources.map((source) => source.url),
    },
    {
      "@type": "DefinedTermSet",
      "@id": `${canonical}#terms`,
      name: "GCash and Philippine payments: terms explained",
      hasDefinedTerm: terms.map((item) => ({
        "@type": "DefinedTerm",
        name: item.term,
        description: item.meaning,
      })),
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": `${canonical}#faq`,
      mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        name: faq.question,
        acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: faq.answer },
      })),
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "@id": `${canonical}#breadcrumb`,
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${site}/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Payment solutions", item: `${site}/payment-solutions/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Case studies", item: `${site}/payment-solutions/case-studies/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 4, name: guide.title, item: canonical },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

const emailHref = `mailto:matteo.visibility@gmail.com?subject=${encodeURIComponent("GCash payments")}`;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} image="/images/case-studies/lofoten-coast-og.jpg">
  <Header />

  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />

  <main class="cs-page">
    <CaseStudyHero
      crumbLabel="Guides"
      crumbHref="/payment-solutions/case-studies/#guides"
      title={guide.title}
      titleId="article-title"
      variant="article"
    >
      Guide · Philippines · <time datetime={guide.published}>{publishedLabel}</time> · {guide.readingMinutes} min read
    </CaseStudyHero>

    <article class="cs-article" aria-labelledby="article-title">
      <p class="cs-dek">
        GCash is how most Filipinos pay online. For a business, it is also a
        payment method where failures are common and hard to explain. This
        guide explains GCash from the start: why it exists, how the money and
        the messages move, how an integration works, and how to find out why a
        payment failed.
      </p>

      <dl class="cs-facts-line">
        <div><dt>For</dt><dd>{guide.audience}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Market</dt><dd>Philippines (PHP)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Level</dt><dd>From zero</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <aside class="cs-note" aria-label="About this guide">
        <strong>About this guide</strong>
        <p>
          This guide uses GCash's Help Center, the documentation of payment
          gateways that offer GCash (Xendit, PayMongo, Adyen, Checkout.com),
          central bank rules and public company data, as of October 2026. The
          API examples are simplified, and field names differ between
          providers. Limits and prices change, so always check the current
          sources. I am not affiliated with GCash or with any gateway.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <section class="cs-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
        <h2 id="summary-title">Summary</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>GCash started in 2004 as a way to send money by SMS to people without bank accounts. Today it is the Philippines' largest wallet, with about 40 million monthly users.</li>
          <li>GCash is a wallet tied to a person's mobile number, with verification levels and limits. This design explains many payment failures.</li>
          <li>Most businesses accept GCash through a payment gateway. The customer is sent to GCash to approve, and the gateway confirms the result with a webhook.</li>
          <li>Failures are hard to diagnose because four parties are involved, the approval happens on the customer's phone, and some failure codes do not give a reason.</li>
          <li>The solution is a method: measure each stage, save every code, look for patterns in time, amount and device, close open payments and escalate with evidence.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <nav class="cs-toc" aria-labelledby="toc-title">
        <h2 id="toc-title">In this guide</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what">What GCash is</a></li>
          <li><a href="#origin">What GCash was created for</a></li>
          <li><a href="#terms">Fourteen terms, with examples</a></li>
          <li><a href="#infrastructure">How the infrastructure works</a></li>
          <li><a href="#integration">How the integration works, step by step</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mistakes">Ten mistakes companies make</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-hard">Why failures are hard to diagnose</a></li>
          <li><a href="#solve">How to diagnose and solve failures</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">What people ask about GCash</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- 1. What -->
      <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-title">
        <h2 id="what-title">What GCash is</h2>
        <p>
          GCash is a mobile wallet. A person installs the app, registers with a
          Philippine mobile number and adds money to it. With that money, the
          person can send money to family, pay bills, buy mobile load, pay in
          shops with a QR code and pay online. Over the years, GCash also added
          savings, loans, insurance and investments, so today it calls itself a
          "finance super app".
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-facts" aria-labelledby="facts-title">
          <figcaption id="facts-title">GCash: key facts</figcaption>
          <dl>
            {
              facts.map((fact) => (
                <div>
                  <dt>{fact.label}</dt>
                  <dd>{fact.value}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Two facts from Mynt's stock market listing are important for any
          business. About 78% of GCash users live outside Metro Manila, and 92%
          belong to lower-income groups. In practice, this means many customers
          pay with small balances, on mobile networks, and sometimes with
          limited accounts. Keep this in mind; it returns in the section on
          failures.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">To understand why GCash works the way it does, we need to go back to why it was created.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 2. Origin -->
      <section id="origin" aria-labelledby="origin-title">
        <h2 id="origin-title">What GCash was created for</h2>
        <p>
          GCash was not created for online shopping. It was created to move
          money between people who had a mobile phone but no bank account.
        </p>
        <ol class="cs-timeline">
          {
            timeline.map((item) => (
              <li>
                <span class="cs-timeline-year">{item.year}</span>
                <p>{item.text}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p>
          This origin explains three things that still matter today. First,
          the account is tied to a <strong>mobile number</strong>, so security
          depends on SMS codes reaching that phone. Second, GCash is
          <strong>regulated as e-money</strong>, so the central bank requires
          identity checks and limits. Third, the wallet was built for
          <strong>small, everyday amounts</strong>, and the limits reflect that.
          A card network was designed for buying; GCash was designed for
          sending. Online payments were added later, on top.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">Before we look at how payments work, here are the terms you will meet in every conversation about GCash.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. Terms -->
      <section id="terms" aria-labelledby="terms-title">
        <h2 id="terms-title">Fourteen terms, with examples</h2>
        <dl class="cs-terms">
          {
            terms.map((item) => (
              <div>
                <dt>{item.term}</dt>
                <dd>
                  <p>{item.meaning}</p>
                  <p class="cs-term-example"><span>Example</span>{item.example}</p>
                </dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
        <p>
          The limits deserve a closer look, because they cause many failures
          that look technical but are not. These are GCash's published limits
          for the three most common profiles:
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-compare">
          <figcaption>GCash limits by profile (selection)</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Profile</th>
                  <th scope="col">Maximum balance</th>
                  <th scope="col">Money in, per month</th>
                  <th scope="col">Money out</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  limits.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{row.profile}</th>
                      <td>{row.wallet}</td>
                      <td>{row.incoming}</td>
                      <td>{row.outgoingMonth}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p>
          GCash also has profiles for minors (GCash Jr.) and for premium users
          (GCash Platinum). When a user reaches a limit, some services stop
          until the first day of the next month, or until the user upgrades.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">With these terms, we can follow a payment through the system.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. Infrastructure -->
      <section id="infrastructure" aria-labelledby="infrastructure-title">
        <h2 id="infrastructure-title">How the infrastructure works</h2>
        <p>
          An online GCash payment involves four parties. Each one sees only its
          own part of the payment, which is important later.
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-parties" aria-labelledby="parties-title">
          <figcaption id="parties-title">The four parties in an online GCash payment</figcaption>
          <ol>
            {
              parties.map((party) => (
                <li>
                  <span class="cs-currency-kind">{party.kind}</span>
                  <strong>{party.name}</strong>
                  <small>{party.detail}</small>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Two things move between these parties, and they move separately.
        </p>
        <ul class="cs-plain-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Messages</strong> move in seconds: your server asks the
            gateway for a payment, the gateway asks GCash, GCash asks the
            customer, and the answers travel back the same way.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Money</strong> moves more slowly: GCash deducts the
            customer's balance immediately, the gateway collects the money, and
            pays it to your bank account days later (settlement), minus its fee.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Around this online flow, the Philippines has national payment rails
          that GCash also uses. <strong>InstaPay</strong> moves money between
          banks and wallets in real time, for example when a customer cashes
          in from a bank. <strong>QR Ph</strong> lets one QR code accept
          payments from any participating wallet or bank app. In July 2026, the
          central bank and the payment industry also launched InstaPay
          Cash-In, InstaPay for Business and Direct Debit PH, which make it
          easier to move money from a bank into a wallet.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, GCash is connected to <strong>Alipay+</strong>, the network
          of Ant Group. Through it, GCash users can pay at Alipay+ merchants
          abroad, and international merchants can accept GCash through
          providers connected to Alipay+.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">Now that the parties are clear, we can follow one integration from the first decision to the first refund.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Integration -->
      <section id="integration" aria-labelledby="integration-title">
        <h2 id="integration-title">How the integration works, step by step</h2>
        <p>
          The example in this section is an online shop that sells phone
          accessories. A customer orders a case and a charger for ₱2,499 and
          chooses GCash.
        </p>

        <div class="cs-point" id="step-1">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Step 1</span> Choose your route to GCash</h3>
          <p>There are three main routes. Most businesses choose the first one.</p>
          <figure class="cs-compare">
            <figcaption>Three ways to accept GCash</figcaption>
            <div class="cs-table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Route</th>
                    <th scope="col">Examples</th>
                    <th scope="col">Best for</th>
                    <th scope="col">What to know</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {
                    routes.map((route) => (
                      <tr>
                        <th scope="row">{route.name}</th>
                        <td>{route.examples}</td>
                        <td>{route.bestFor}</td>
                        <td>{route.notes}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))
                  }
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <p class="cs-bridge">Whichever route you choose, the provider first needs to know your business.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="step-2">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Step 2</span> Complete onboarding</h3>
          <p>
            The provider checks your business (KYB, "know your business"). A
            Philippine business usually provides its registration documents,
            tax registration and a bank account for settlement. After approval,
            GCash must be activated as a payment method. PayMongo, for example,
            says to expect about five business days for GCash activation,
            while test payments are possible immediately.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">While onboarding runs, engineers can build with test keys. The first thing they build is the payment request.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="step-3">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Step 3</span> Create the payment request</h3>
          <p>
            Your server tells the gateway: this order, this amount, in pesos,
            with GCash, and where to send the customer afterwards. The example
            follows Xendit's documentation; other gateways use similar fields.
          </p>
          <figure class="cs-code">
            <figcaption>Create a GCash payment request</figcaption>
            <pre><code>{codeCreate}</code></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>
            Three details matter. The <code>currency</code> must be PHP. The
            <code>reference_id</code> must be your own order number, so you can
            connect every answer to the right order. And the two return
            addresses decide where the customer lands, but they do not prove
            anything (see step 6).
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">The gateway answers immediately, but the answer is not "paid". It is "the customer must act".</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="step-4">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Step 4</span> Send the customer to GCash</h3>
          <figure class="cs-code">
            <figcaption>The gateway's answer (simplified)</figcaption>
            <pre><code>{codeResponse}</code></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>
            The status <code>REQUIRES_ACTION</code> means the customer must
            approve. Your checkout sends the customer to the address in the
            answer. On a computer, this is a web page. On a phone, Xendit uses a
            deep link that opens the GCash app.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">From this moment, the payment happens outside your website, on the customer's phone.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="step-5">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Step 5</span> The customer approves in GCash</h3>
          <p>According to Adyen's description of the flow, the customer:</p>
          <ol class="cs-plain-list cs-plain-list--numbered">
            <li>logs in to GCash with their mobile number;</li>
            <li>enters the one-time password (OTP) received by SMS;</li>
            <li>enters their MPIN;</li>
            <li>confirms the payment and returns to your site;</li>
            <li>receives an SMS confirmation from GCash.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Each of these steps can fail: the OTP arrives late, the MPIN is
            wrong, the balance is too low, or the time runs out. The time limit
            depends on the provider: 15 minutes with Xendit and 30 minutes by
            default with Adyen. With PayMongo's older Sources flow, your system
            must create the payment within one hour of the customer's approval,
            or the money returns to the customer.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">When the customer is done, two signals come back: the customer's browser and the webhook. Only one of them is reliable.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="step-6">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Step 6</span> Wait for the webhook</h3>
          <p>
            The webhook is the gateway's message to your server. It says what
            really happened. Here is a success and a failure:
          </p>
          <figure class="cs-code">
            <figcaption>Webhook: payment succeeded (simplified)</figcaption>
            <pre><code>{codeWebhookSuccess}</code></pre>
          </figure>
          <figure class="cs-code">
            <figcaption>Webhook: payment failed (simplified)</figcaption>
            <pre><code>{codeWebhookFailure}</code></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>A good webhook handler:</p>
          <ol class="cs-plain-list cs-plain-list--numbered">
            <li>checks that the message really comes from the gateway (Xendit, for example, sends a callback token that you compare with your own);</li>
            <li>finds the order with the <code>reference_id</code>;</li>
            <li>processes each event only once, even if it arrives twice;</li>
            <li>saves the status and, for failures, the <code>failure_code</code>;</li>
            <li>answers the gateway quickly.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Xendit also notes that some channels do not send a failure
            notification at all. That is why step 8 exists.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">Once the order is confirmed, the next event in its life may be a refund.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="step-7">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Step 7</span> Handle refunds</h3>
          <p>
            Refunds are created through the gateway and return the money to
            the customer's GCash wallet. With Xendit and PayMongo, GCash refunds
            are possible for up to 180 days, and partial refunds are allowed.
            PayMongo says the refund reflects in the customer's wallet within 24
            hours. Your support team should know these rules, because customers
            who ask GCash about a refund are sent back to the merchant.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">Refunds and payments both end in the same place: your bank account and your books.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point" id="step-8">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Step 8</span> Close open payments and reconcile</h3>
          <p>
            Every day, compare three lists: your orders, the gateway's payments
            and the money received in your bank. In addition, run a job that
            checks every payment that is still open after its expiry, asks the
            gateway for the final status, and updates the order. Settlement
            timing depends on the provider: Xendit lists T+2 calendar days for
            GCash, and PayMongo 2 to 7 days.
          </p>
          <figure class="cs-compare">
            <figcaption>GCash rules at three providers (as published, October 2026)</figcaption>
            <div class="cs-table-wrap">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Rule</th>
                    <th scope="col">Xendit</th>
                    <th scope="col">Adyen</th>
                    <th scope="col">PayMongo</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {
                    providerFacts.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
                        <td>{row.xendit}</td>
                        <td>{row.adyen}</td>
                        <td>{row.paymongo}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))
                  }
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <p class="cs-bridge">The steps are simple on paper. In practice, companies make the same mistakes again and again.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 6. Mistakes -->
      <section id="mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-title">
        <h2 id="mistakes-title">Ten mistakes companies make</h2>
        <ol class="cs-steps">
          {
            mistakes.map((mistake) => (
              <li>
                <h3>{mistake.title}</h3>
                <p>{mistake.text}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="cs-bridge">Most of these mistakes have one thing in common: they make failures harder to see. The next section explains why failures are hard to see in the first place.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 7. Why hard -->
      <section id="why-hard" aria-labelledby="why-hard-title">
        <h2 id="why-hard-title">Why GCash failures are hard to diagnose</h2>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Reason 1</span> Four parties, four sets of records</h3>
          <p>
            The customer sees their app, GCash sees its ledger, the gateway sees
            its requests, and you see your orders. Nobody sees everything. You
            usually see only what the gateway tells you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Reason 2</span> The important part happens on the customer's phone</h3>
          <p>
            The OTP, the MPIN, the balance check and the confirmation happen
            outside your website. If an OTP does not arrive because of a weak
            signal or a full SMS inbox, which GCash's own help pages mention,
            your system only sees a payment that was never approved.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Reason 3</span> Abandonment looks like failure</h3>
          <p>
            A customer who changes their mind and a customer who tried but
            could not pay can produce the same result: an expired request. If
            you count both as "failed payments", the number is too high and the
            causes are mixed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Reason 4</span> Some codes do not explain anything</h3>
          <p>
            Codes such as <code>FAILURE_DETAILS_UNAVAILABLE</code> (Xendit) mean
            that GCash reported a failure without a reason. PayMongo's
            <code>CLOSED</code> can mean an expired checkout or a general error
            from GCash. With these codes, you need patterns, not single cases.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Reason 5</span> Timing creates contradictions</h3>
          <p>
            A customer can see "deducted" in GCash while your order still says
            "pending". GCash's Help Center has whole articles for this
            situation: "wallet deducted, but the merchant did not receive the
            payment". Usually the payment is still processing, or it will be
            reversed, but until the final status arrives both sides tell a
            different story.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Reason 6</span> Short incidents and planned maintenance</h3>
          <p>
            GCash announces maintenance windows on its Help Center, usually at
            night. Gateways also have short incidents with their GCash
            connection. A 30-minute problem on a busy evening can look like a
            sudden drop in conversion, with no error in your own systems.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="cs-bridge">None of these reasons can be removed. But each one can be measured, and measured problems can be solved.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 8. Solve -->
      <section id="solve" aria-labelledby="solve-title">
        <h2 id="solve-title">How to diagnose and solve GCash failures</h2>
        <p>
          The first tool is a table that connects each failure code to its
          meaning and to the party that can fix it.
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-compare">
          <figcaption>Common GCash failure codes at two gateways, and who can fix them</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table class="cs-table-codes">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Code</th>
                  <th scope="col">What it means</th>
                  <th scope="col">Who can fix it</th>
                  <th scope="col">What you do</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  codes.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{row.code.split(", ").map((code) => <code>{code}</code>)}<small>{row.source}</small></th>
                      <td>{row.meaning}</td>
                      <td>{row.owner}</td>
                      <td>{row.action}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p>
          With that table in place, follow these eight steps. They work for
          one bad day and for a problem that has lasted months.
        </p>
        <ol class="cs-steps">
          {
            diagnosisSteps.map((step) => (
              <li>
                <h3>{step.title}</h3>
                <p>{step.text}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="cs-takeaway">
          A GCash failure is rarely "GCash is broken". It is usually one of
          four things: the customer's balance or limits, the time to approve,
          the device and redirect, or a short incident. Measure the stages,
          keep the codes, and the cause becomes visible.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">These are also the questions that customers and businesses ask most often about GCash.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section id="faq" class="cs-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
        <h2 id="faq-title">What people ask about GCash</h2>
        <p>
          The questions below are the ones that appear most often in GCash's
